// Writing an erased `T` field across a module boundary (task 100.4.3.10 — the write dual of the erased
// field read/return path). The producer compiles each method once erased over `T`; the store into
// `value` is a VWT-sized copy from the argument buffer, not a first-class pointer store, so the write
// lands in the field's bytes.
//
// `Box<T>` is a **value** (struct) receiver: its mutating-ness crosses in the `.nmi`, so the consumer
// passes `self` by its real storage and the mutation sticks past the call. `Ref<T>` is a **reference**
// (class) receiver: `self` is the shared object, so the write is visible through any handle.
public struct Box<T> {
    var value: T
    fun get() -> T { return value }
    fun replace(x: T) { value = x }
}

public class Ref<T> {
    var value: T
    fun get() -> T { return value }
    fun replace(x: T) { value = x }
}
